# Copy this file to `.env` (same folder) and fill in your secrets.
# `.env` is gitignored — it will NOT be committed.
#
# Alternatively, put these in ~/.crew/env to share across all projects
# (recommended for the launchd/cron setup, since launchd ignores your shell rc).

# --- Tracker credentials ---------------------------------------------------
# Fill in the block matching `tracker.provider` in config.yaml. Delete the other.

# provider: linear
# Linear personal API key: Linear → Settings → Security & access → Personal API keys
LINEAR_API_KEY=lin_api_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# provider: jira  (Jira Cloud)
# Token: id.atlassian.com → Security → Create and manage API tokens
#
# A Jira API token has NO scopes of its own — it inherits every permission of
# the account that created it. So create it from a dedicated service account
# holding only these project permissions, rather than from an admin account:
#   Browse Projects     read the board (required by everything else)
#   Create Issues       file proposals
#   Edit Issues         update fields
#   Transition Issues   move items between statuses
#   Assign Issues       assign work to itself
#   Add Comments        post PR links and review notes
#   Link Issues         link PRDs to the work they spawn
# The account must also hold "Assignable User" in the project to be assigned to.
# Tokens expire (1 year by default) — expect a hard 401 when one lapses.
# JIRA_HOST=your-site.atlassian.net
# JIRA_EMAIL=you@example.com
# JIRA_API_TOKEN=ATATT3xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- Code host -------------------------------------------------------------
# Only needed when `repo.forge` is "bitbucket". GitHub needs nothing here — the
# `gh` CLI carries its own auth (run `gh auth login`).
#
# RECOMMENDED — a Repository Access Token (not tied to any person, so it keeps
# working when someone leaves). Repository settings → Access tokens.
# Scopes to tick:
#   pullrequest:write   open PRs and comment on them
#   repository:write    push the branch  (skip if you push over SSH)
# BITBUCKET_ACCESS_TOKEN=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#
# ALTERNATIVE — an Atlassian API token with scopes, paired with your account
# EMAIL (not your username). id.atlassian.com → Security → API tokens.
# Scopes to tick — Bitbucket scopes do NOT imply each other, so tick all of:
#   read:repository:bitbucket     read the repo
#   write:repository:bitbucket    push the branch  (skip if you push over SSH)
#   read:pullrequest:bitbucket    comment on PRs
#   write:pullrequest:bitbucket   open PRs
# BITBUCKET_EMAIL=you@example.com
# BITBUCKET_API_TOKEN=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#
# App passwords (BITBUCKET_USERNAME + BITBUCKET_APP_PASSWORD) were REMOVED by
# Atlassian on 2026-07-28 and now fail with 401. Use one of the two above.

# --- Agent -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Only needed when `agent.provider` is "claude". Other CLIs use their own auth.
# Long-lived Claude token for headless runs: generate with `claude setup-token`
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# --- External tools (MCP) --------------------------------------------------
# Credentials for anything under `mcpServers:` in config.yaml. That file is
# committed and holds only ${PLACEHOLDERS}; the real values live here. crew
# resolves them at spawn time into a temp file outside the repo, hands it to the
# agent, and deletes it when the run ends.
#
# A variable that's referenced but unset SKIPS that agent's run with a message
# naming it — nothing runs half-configured against a broken tool.
#
# Grant read-only scopes. Agents propose work; the engine files it. A token that
# can write to your tracker lets an agent bypass dedup and the PRD gate.
#
# Example — Sentry, so QA files bugs that are actually firing in production.
# Create at: Sentry → Settings → Auth Tokens. Scopes: project:read, event:read
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=sntrys_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
#
# Example — an analytics MCP server behind a bearer token.
# ANALYTICS_TOKEN=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
